[you installed it on the network, but play it at some other guys computer,
then discover that he is cheating -- what to do?]

First, who is he cheating?  I like option 3, but just delete the high
score list (or just his scores from it) and tell him (if he asks) that it
is only for real games. What is he going to do?  He will still be able to
cheat his heart out (which is not a crime) and no one else will have to
see his name on the list undeservedly.  It is not so much the cheating,
but the passing it off as equal to the games where cheating didn't occur
that is the real problem here. 

But your third option does open the debate on how best to sabotage
a person's game.  I like what I've heard so far, but then when is sabotage
of this magnitude called for?  Physical violence would not be out of the
question for someone who put a game that I had spent alot of time on into
discovery mode -- for any reason.

